## RESULTS AND DISCUSSION
The Principle of the Biosensor. In this study, a label-free electrochemical biosensor for the detection of GBM derived exosomes has been proposed and the principle of the design is depicted in Scheme 1. Firstly, \(\mathrm{UiO}{-}66{-}\mathrm{NH}_{2}\), a typical Zr-MOF, is chosen and synthesized through a reported method with slight modification. Then, the MB@UiO-66-based nanoprobe is generated by stirring MB and \(\mathrm{UiO}{-}66{-}\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) at room temperature. As well known, MB is an electroactive redox indicator which is widely used to produce electrochemical signals on the electrode surface. The high porosity of MOFs can load large numbers of MB, thus resulting in a high signal and improving the sensitivity of the biosensor. In the meantime, a designed peptide (H-C-acp-acp-FALGEA- \(\cdot\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) ) containing a protein binding motif is assembled as the sensing layer on the electrode surface by formation of Au-S bond through the cysteine and
gold electrode. The peptide is elongated at N-terminal with 6-aminocaproic acid to increase its flexibility, and amination is designed at C-terminal to reduce the electrostatic adsorption. As results, the biosensor can be employed for the quantitative analysis of exosomes, sensitively, quickly and simply. Consequently, the peptide can selectively recognize EGFR/EGFRvIII and capture GBM-derived exosomes through the specific molecular recognition. After that, the functional MB@Zr-MOFs will combine with GBM-derived exosomes and produce a highly amplified electrochemical signal by the interaction between \(\mathrm{Zr}^{4+}\) and the phosphate head of exosomes.
Characterization of MOFs. The morphology and structure of \(\mathrm{UiO}{-}66{-}\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) is characterized by scanning electron microscope (SEM) and X-ray diffraction (XRD). As illustrated in Fig. 1A, 1C, the size of MOF is about 100 nm with uniform shape, and the sharp diffraction peaks of XRD are consistent with the simulated, which reveal the successfully synthesis of the MOF. After electrochemical dyes are loaded into \(\mathrm{UiO}{-}66{-}\mathrm{NH}_{2}\), the SEM results are basically the same except for the color change (Fig. 1B). The XRD data preserve all diffraction peaks, suggesting that loading of dyes makes little influence on the crystalline structure. In order to further verify the successful fabrication of the nanoprobe, adsorption-desorption isotherm is performed at \(77\,\mathrm{~K~}\) (Fig. 1D). It can be calculated that the
Brunauer-Emmett-Teller surface area of UiO-66 is \(643~\mathrm{m}^{2}/\mathrm{g}\) while the \(\mathrm{MB}@\mathrm{UiO}{-}66\) is \(227~\mathrm{m}^{2}/\mathrm{g}\), which confirms that MBs are effectively encapsulated. In addition, Fourier transform infrared spectroscopy (FT-IR) has been conducted to investigate the variation (Fig. S1). The characteristic peak at \(1593~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\) originating from the vibrations of \(\mathrm{C}{=}\mathrm{C}\) in the benzene ring is enhanced, indicating the increase of the
aromatic compounds. Besides, new characteristic peaks at \(1343~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\), \(948~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\), and \(886~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}\) appear in the \(\mathrm{MB}@\mathrm{UiO}{-}66\), which prove that MB is encapsulated in \(\mathrm{UiO}{-}66{-}\mathrm{NH}_{2}\).<sup>51</sup> Moreover, the amount of MB in the MOF has been determined by UV/Vis spectroscopy and the loading rate is \(65.35\%\) (Fig. S9).<sup>52</sup>
Characterization of Exosomes. Exosomes pellets are isolated from the U87 GBM cells supernatant based on the differential centrifugation method. The physical characteristics are measured by NanoSight, revealing that the concentration is \(\sim1.9\!\times\!10^{7}\) particles \(\mathrm{\Delta\muL}\) with a size of about \(80\ \mathrm{nm}\) (Fig. S2A). Transmission electron microscope (TEM) image has also been performed to examine the morphology, showing a typical membrane-bound vesicle with the diameters consistent with the size of NanoSight (Fig. S2B). These results corroborate with previously reported evidence, suggesting that the isolated exosomes can be used for the biosensor construction.
Feasibility Validation of the Biosensor. The electrochemical biosensor has been firstly investigated by electrochemical impedance spectroscopy (EIS) with \(\mathrm{Fe}(\mathrm{CN})_{6}{}^{3-/4-}\) redox couple. As shown in Fig. 2A, the bare gold electrode exhibits similar to a straight line (curve a), implying little electron transfer resistance. However, after the electrode is modified step-by-step with peptide, exosomes, and \(\mathrm{MB}@\mathrm{UiO}{-}66\), the diameter of semicircles increase gradually (curve b-d). As the diameter of the semicircle represents the electron transfer resistance at the electrode interface, it is clearly confirmed the process of electrode modification and the binding of exosomes with peptide, as well \(\mathrm{MB}@\mathrm{UiO}{-}66\). Secondly, the square wave voltammograms (SWV) are measured in the Tris-HCl buffer to verify the feasibility and specificity of this biosensor. As shown in Fig. 2B, only small current signals are observed when applying SWV scan to the bare electrode (curve a) or exosomes-free electrode (curve b), indicating that there is no electron communication at the electrode surface. While a significant peak can be detected in the present of exosomes under the same condition (curve c), which confirm the viability of this biosensor for detection of exosomes.
Optimization of Experimental Conditions. In order to achieve the best performance of the biosensor for exosomes detection, the effects of corresponding experimental conditions on the biosensor have been optimized by conducting the following experiments. First, the concentration and incubation time of peptide have been optimized, since they are the important consideration in the modification of electrode surface. The results demonstrate that the highest electrochemical signal can be detected by
using \(5~\upmu\mathrm{M}\) peptide after incubation for \(16~\mathrm{h}\) (Fig.S3, S4). The peptide-functionalized electrode is then used to capture exosomes and the optimized time is shown to be \(2\,\mathrm{~h~}\) (Fig.S5). Under the optimum conditions of the modification of electrode surface, we investigate the reaction time and the amounts of \(\mathrm{MB}@\mathrm{UiO}{-}66\) by detecting \(1.9\times10^{7}\) particles \(\mathrm{\Delta\muL}\) exosomes using the biosensor. With the prolonging of incubation time of \(\mathrm{MB}@\mathrm{UiO}{-}66\), the current intensity increases continuously and almost reaches a plateau at 30 min (Fig.3A, 3B). So, \(30\,\mathrm{\min}\) is chosen as the optimal incubation time. Then, the concentration of \(\mathrm{MB}@\mathrm{UiO}{-}66\) is optimized by binding with exosomes or exosomes-free electrode for \(30~\mathrm{\min}\). The SWV results indicate that \(0.5\;\mathrm{mg/mL}\) of \(\mathrm{MB}@\mathrm{UiO}{-}66\) can provide high signal readout with low background (Fig. 3C, 3D). Therefore, \(0.5\;\mathrm{mg/mL}\) is chosen as the optimal concentration of \(\mathrm{MB}@\mathrm{UiO}{-}66\) for the following experiments.
Performance of the Biosensor. Under the optimized experimental conditions, the fabricated biosensor has been employed for the quantitative analysis of GBMs-derived exosomes. SWV measurement is carried out and the results of exosomes analysis at different concentrations are displayed in Fig. 4A. With the amounts of exosomes increasing, the electrochemical currents enhance gradually. A linear relationship can be established between the peak currents and the logarithm of exosomes concentrations ranging from \(9.5\times10^{3}\) to \(1.9\times10^{7}\) particles \(\mathrm{\Delta\muL}\), with a linear equation of \(y=-5.32+1.36\mathrm{~x~}\) (Fig. 4B). It can be estimated that the \(7.83\ \times10^{3}\) particles \(\mathrm{\Delta\muL}\) is the lowest detected concentration, which equal to the sum of background signal and three standard deviations \((\mathrm{y}=0.892~\upmu\mathrm{A}+3~\times0.045~\upmu\mathrm{A}\) \(=1.027~\upmu\mathrm{A})\). Meanwhile, the limit of detection (LOD) of our proposed biosensor has been compared with other previous reports, indicating its high sensitivity and large linear range for exosomes detection (Table S1). The selectivity of this method is further demonstrated by detecting same numbers of exosomes derived from different cells (Fig. S7). The reproducibility of electrochemical biosensor is conducted by detecting exosomes at the concentration of \(9.5\,\times\,10^{5}\) particles \(\mathrm{\Delta\muL}\) (Fig. S8), and the value of the relative standard deviation is \(2.34\%\) \((\mathfrak{n}=6)\). Moreover, exosomes spiked in human serum are also challenged. As shown in Fig. S6, under the same
experimental conditions, the signal responses of exosomes diluted into serum are almost consistent with that in buffer, indicating that the biological matrix environment has little effect on the sensor. These results suggest that the biosensor can work well in the complex biological samples. Significantly, the GBM-derived exosomes in human serum can be detected by our sensor, as demonstrated in the following study.
Clinical Sample Analysis. To test the clinically diagnosis application of our biosensor, human serum are collected from 24 clinical samples of 8 healthy individuals (HS), 8 patients with GBM (GBM), and 8 patients after surgery (GBM-AS). As shown in Fig. 5A, current signals of exosomes from healthy serum are significantly lower than GBMs patients and postoperative, consistent with the published report that a higher expression of EGFR/ EGFRvIII in GBM-derived exosomes than normal cells.53 The statistical analysis of these results is also conducted and the scattered dot plots clearly show that there is a significant difference between the healthy group and GBM patients (Fig. 5B). Notably, the measured SWV currents of exosomes in GBM-AS are less than those before surgery, indicating the capacity of our sensor for pre-and post-surgical monitoring of GBM patients. These results not only verify that the EGFR/ EGFRvIII positive exosomes in serum can be used as biomarkers but also demonstrate the capacity of our sensor for early diagnosis, pre-and post-surgical monitoring of GBM patients.
## CONCLUSIONS
In conclusion, we have successfully developed a label-free electrochemical biosensor for the detection of GBM-derived exosomes by using the Zr-based Metal-Organic Frameworks. In the design of the biosensor, a peptide can selectively capture GBMs-derived exosomes through the highly expressed EGFR and EGFRvIII proteins. Meanwhile, based on the combination between \( Z\mathrm{r}^{4+} \) and the exosomes surface, Zr-MOFs loaded with methylene blue may approach to the electrode surface to generate a high electrochemical signal. Therefore, the concentration of exosomes can be directly quantified without extra recognition and amplification elements, while the sensor can show very high selectivity and sensitivity. Moreover, this biosensor has been employed to analyze GBM-derived exosomes in human serum and distinguish GBM patients from healthy groups, which has demonstrated the feasibility for early-diagnosis and monitoring in GBM therapy. Furthermore, the strategy of the design can be to detect other disease biomarkers by simply replacing the capture sequences, showing the great prospective values to fabricate other clinical sensing platform.
